Here, the image with a blue color channel is stored as b, the image with a green color channel as g, and the image with a red color channel as r. When we run the code, we can see that the image stored as b shows a grayscale image. In this image, the areas that are initially blue are light, and the areas that weren’t blue are dark. The blue channel matrix contains the blue color intensity values, as integers, for all the image pixels. All the other images are displayed similarly as per their color.

Merge color channels
To merge the color channels, we use the cv2.merge() method of the OpenCV library. This method requires three different color channels as the parameter of the function:

merged = cv2.merge([b,g,r])
cv2.imshow('Merged Image', merged)
Note: We have to keep the images of color channels inside a square bracket.

